insert into public.journal_posts
  (slug, title, category, published_on, read_time, image, excerpt, featured, body)
values
  ('make-your-bouquet-last-longer', 'How to make your bouquet last longer', 'flower-care', '2026-07-11', '4 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1526047932273-341f2a7631f9?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'Small habits — fresh water, an angled cut, a cool spot away from sunlight — make the biggest difference.', true, ARRAY['A bouquet is a living thing for the first week of its life, and how you treat those seven days decides everything. The good news: the habits that keep flowers beautiful are small, cheap, and take about a minute each morning.', 'Start with the cut. Trim every stem at an angle the moment it arrives — a clean 45-degree cut opens the water channel so the stem can drink. Do the same whenever you change the water, which should be every two days. Use sharp scissors or a knife; crushing stems with blunt blades does more harm than good.', 'Then the water itself. Use clean, room-temperature water and strip any leaves that would sit below the waterline — submerged leaves rot and feed bacteria. Keep the vase out of direct sun and away from heat sources. A cool room is a flower’s best friend.']),
  ('whats-in-season-this-month', 'What’s in season this month', 'seasonal', '2026-06-27', '3 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1470509037663-253afd7f0f51?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'A look at the blooms currently shaping our arrangements, and why we let the season lead.', true, ARRAY['The season is the only true source of truth in floristry. What is blooming right now is, by definition, what will look best on your table — because it is at its peak, grown locally, and priced honestly.', 'This month the worktable is full of field flowers and loose, generous stems: cosmos, grasses, and the last of the sunflowers. They are the kind of blooms that refuse to look arranged, which is exactly what we want.', 'When you choose with the season, you get more flower for the same money, a longer vase life, and a bouquet that actually belongs to the moment it was given. That is the whole philosophy, in one line.']),
  ('inside-the-atelier', 'Inside the atelier: a quiet morning', 'behind-the-scenes', '2026-06-01', '5 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1465146344425-f00d5f5c8f07?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'From stem selection to the final wrap — a walk through a morning at the ESENEL worktable.', true, ARRAY['The atelier opens with the market, not with us. By the time the first light hits the worktable, the day’s stems have already been chosen — picked for shape and character rather than uniformity, the way a gardener would choose for their own table.', 'Each order is arranged the morning it goes out. We cut close to the delivery window, build the composition stem by stem, and stop while it still breathes. The restraint is deliberate: an over-filled bouquet reads as effort, not as care.', 'The wrap is the last act — paper, ribbon, a handwritten note tucked inside. By midday the first deliveries are on their way, and the worktable is clean again, waiting for tomorrow’s stems.']),
  ('choosing-flowers-as-a-gift', 'Choosing flowers as a gift', 'gift-guides', '2026-05-18', '4 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1487530811176-3780de880c2d?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'A short guide to picking an arrangement that fits the person, not just the occasion.', true, ARRAY['Most people buy flowers for an occasion and end up buying for the occasion itself. But the arrangements people remember are the ones that feel chosen for them — their colours, their scale, their way of being.', 'Start with the person. Do they keep things minimal or maximal? A single stem in a narrow vase can say more than a full bouquet to the right person. Then think about the space — a small bedside table wants a compact arrangement, a dining table can carry something generous.', 'Finally, the occasion matters less than the timing. Flowers given on an ordinary Tuesday land harder than flowers given on a schedule. The bouquet is the excuse; the thought is the gift.']),
  ('five-minute-bouquet-refresh', 'The five-minute bouquet refresh', 'flower-care', '2026-05-03', '3 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1490750967868-88aa4486c946?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'Your bouquet doesn’t need a florist to come back to life — it needs five minutes and clean water.', false, ARRAY['Day three and the bouquet is drooping? Before you blame the flowers, try the refresh. Remove the arrangement from the vase, re-cut every stem at an angle, and rinse the vase with warm soapy water.', 'Fill it with fresh, room-temperature water and add nothing else — home remedies like sugar or aspirin feed bacteria more than they feed flowers. Then return the stems, arranging them loosely so air can move through the composition.', 'Five minutes later the bouquet will stand straighter, drink properly, and buy itself another three or four days. That is the whole trick: clean water, a fresh cut, and a little patience.']),
  ('why-we-work-with-the-season', 'Why we work with the season', 'seasonal', '2026-04-14', '4 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1520763185298-1b434c919102?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'No frozen imports, no out-of-season stems — here is why the season always leads.', false, ARRAY['It would be easier to offer every flower, every month. It would also mean flying stems halfway across the world, picking them before they are ready, and delivering a bouquet that dies in two days. That trade has never made sense to us.', 'The season gives us what is genuinely good: flowers at their peak, grown close to home, priced without the import markup. It also gives the work character — a June bouquet is not a January bouquet wearing a disguise.', 'So we let the calendar lead. When a flower leaves the season, it leaves the menu. The garden decides what is beautiful this month, and we simply follow.']),
  ('the-craft-table-explained', 'The craft table, explained', 'behind-the-scenes', '2026-03-26', '5 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1487070183336-b863922373d4?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'Size, flowers, wrapping — how the ESENEL craft builder works, and why every choice matters.', false, ARRAY['The craft table is our way of letting you be the designer. You choose the size, the flowers, and the wrapping; we handle the composition, the cut, and the delivery. It is collaboration with a clear division of labour.', 'Size sets the scale of the gesture — from a compact posy for a bedside table to a generous arrangement built to fill a room. Flowers set the character: each stem you add changes the shape, the scent, and the story.', 'Wrapping is the last decision and the first impression. A crisp white paper reads quietly; ink paper makes a statement before the bouquet is even open. Whatever you choose, it is finished by hand and delivered on schedule.']),
  ('a-sleman-wedding-board', 'A Sleman wedding board', 'gift-guides', '2026-03-07', '6 min read', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1509927083803-4bd519298ac4?q=80&w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop', 'How we build a flower board for a Sleman wedding — from the first sketch to the last stem.', false, ARRAY['Weddings in Sleman are loud, generous, and full of colour — the flower board has to keep up without overwhelming the room. We start with a palette drawn from the couple’s story, not from trend pages.', 'The board is built flat on the worktable, flowers arranged in loose clusters with dried material for texture. It is designed to photograph well and to last: boards are made to be kept, not thrown away the morning after.', 'Every board leaves the atelier with a care note and an offer to refresh it after the event. Most couples take us up on it — which is how a wedding board becomes a piece of the home.']);
